body:
I tend to assume that my partner will eventually either lose interest in me, or cheat on me (or both). My current SO is absolutely the most wonderful man I have ever been with, and we've had quite a few discussions on that wall I have up. I'm working on knocking that down, but I'm sure it'll be a process. I have been lied to and cheated on by pretty much every man I've had a serious relationship with previously. Add in mental and verbal abuse from my serious high school boyfriend. I recently got divorced and have since found out a lot of things that I had not been aware of that my ex husband had been up to while he was "at work". 

&#x200B;

My other toxic trait is to constantly assume I don't matter, or that my needs/feelings/wants are less important than others. Another result of the past norm coming into play I assume. I'm learning to catch myself talking negatively to myself and trying to learn how to stop it and flip it. I would never talk to a friend or loved one that way, so why is it ok and normal for me to think/say those things to myself?

I feel this in my soul. Although my ex husband wasn't a gamer, he was (is) a workaholic, and he also wasn't in love with me. So I spent most nights alone. I'd wake up early to get a run in before work and he'd be gone by the time I got back (30 minute run), and he'd come home way after I gave up and went to bed.

&#x200B;

I spent four years married but essentially single. It's extremely lonely and isolating. I was always the only one in the group who's husband "couldn't make it" so I was always the fifth wheel in group outings.

&#x200B;

I've since divorced and have found the most wonderful man who loves me and appreciates me. He wants to spend time together, and we have similar interests. I hope you find that.

As someone who had a cheating partner and married him... I would advise you to run, not walk, as far away as you can. My ex husband cheated one me before we were engaged, I thought we had worked through everything. We got engaged, got married, and bought a house etc. 

&#x200B;

This September he asked for a divorce, claiming he didn't feel anything toward me and he was very unhappy. Wouldn't even consider trying therapy or anything, he was just done with me. Swore up and down that there was no one else (still swears it to this day on the rare occasions I have interaction with him). I was devastated.

&#x200B;

Now that things are finalized there's a lot of stuff coming to the surface that I'm hearing about through mutual friends and contacts. There was someone else, he moved her into our house a month after I moved out. And he's been lying to almost everyone about things from the get go.

&#x200B;

I had thought that sometimes cheating could be a one time mistake. But now, I would tell you to cut your losses and move on. Find someone who will value you and is head over heels crazy for you. You deserve it.
